evidence\ai\transcripts\dean-feldman\2026-08-26-issue-255-competition-scoped-submitter-access.jsonl:600:{"timestamp":"2026-08-26T18:32:38.718Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a03f58-7c3e-7300-9996-c68c23700619","call_id":"call_lZoyX07JYt26JOERiq0ZTt5t","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 2.0 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"FILE docs/security/authentication.md\r\n 225: The backend uses the authenticated and synchronized `app_user` account rather than accepting an account identifier from the client.\r\n 226: \r\n 227: Eligible state transitions are:\r\n 228: \r\n 229: ```text\r\n 230: not_requested -> pending\r\n 231: rejected      -> pending\r\n 232: ```\r\n 233: \r\n 234: An existing `pending` request is rejected with `409 Conflict`, preventing duplicate active requests.\r\n 235: \r\n 236: An account that already has the `submitter` or `admin` role is rejected with `409 Conflict` because\r\n 237: no additional request is necessary. A legacy `approved` request state is also rejected while the\r\n 238: deprecated request workflow remains in place.\r\n 239: \r\n 240: The state transition is performed with a conditional PostgreSQL update so that concurrent duplicate requests cannot both create a new active request.\r\n 241: \r\n 242: The signed-in Account page loads `/api/v1/auth/me` whenever it mounts and displays the persisted\r\n 243: state. Viewer accounts in `not_requested` and `rejected` receive the request action, while `pending`\r\n 244: viewers see an awaiting-review state without another action. Accounts with `submitter` or `admin`\r\n 245: receive a link to the scoped submission interface regardless of the deprecated request state. The\r\n 246: request action has explicit progress, success and error feedback. After a\r\n 247: successful request, or a `409 Conflict` caused by a stale eligible view, the frontend reloads the\r\n 248: current-user profile so refreshes and later authenticated sessions continue from server-owned\r\n 249: state.\r\n 250: \r\n 251: Administrator role assignment and competition-scope assignment remain server-owned operations.\r\n 252: The administrator update endpoint enforces these review transitions while holding a database lock:\r\n 253: \r\n 254: ```text\r\n 255: pending viewer     -> approved submitter\r\n 256: pending viewer     -> rejected viewer\r\n 257: approved submitter -> approved submitter with replacement scope\r\n 258: approved submitter -> viewer with no scope (request decision remains approved)\r\n 259: ```\r\n 260: \r\n 261: Approval and rejection are permitted only from `pending`. Scope replacement and revocation are\r\n 262: permitted only for an existing approved submitter. Other lifecycle changes receive `409 Conflict`\r\n 263: with `INVALID_SUBMITTER_ACCESS_TRANSITION`, so hiding frontend controls is never the authorization\r\n 264: boundary. A rejected viewer must create a new request to return to `pending` before approval.\r\n 265: \r\n 266: Protected routes compose reusable middleware in this order:\r\n 267: \r\n 268: ```ts\r\n 269: requireAuthentication(verifyAccessToken, synchronizeAccount);\r\n 270: requireSubmitter();\r\n 271: requireCompetitionScope((request) => request.params.competitionId);\r\n 272: ```\r\n 273: \r\n 274: Administrator routes use `requireAdministrator()`. Competition resolvers may be asynchronous so a\r\n 275: future fixture or submission route can load the trusted target competition before checking scope.\r\n 276: Missing or invalid credentials return `401`; authenticated but disabled, incorrectly roled, or\r\n 277: out-of-scope accounts return the same non-disclosing `403` response.\r\n 278: \r\n 279: ## Protected endpoint\r\n 280: \r\n 281: ### Request\r\n 282: \r\n 283: ```http\r\n 284: GET /api/v1/auth/me\r\n 285: Authorization: Bearer <supabase-access-token>\r\n 286: ```\r\n 287: \r\n 288: ### Successful response\r\n 289: \r\n 290: ```http\r\n 291: HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\n 292: ```\r\n 293: \r\n 294: ```json\r\n 295: {\r\n 296:   \"user\": {\r\n 297:     \"id\": \"42\",\r\n 298:     \"subject\": \"<supabase-user-id>\",\r\n 299:     \"displayName\": \"Example User\",\r\n 300:     \"role\": \"viewer\",\r\n 301:     \"approvalState\": \"not_requested\",\r\n 302:     \"competitionIds\": []\r\n 303:   }\r\n 304: }\r\n 305: ```\r\n 306: \r\n 307: ### Missing or invalid token\r\n 308: \r\n 309: ```http\r\n 310: HTTP/1.1 401 Unauthorized\r\n 311: WWW-Authenticate: Bearer\r\n 312: ```\r\n 313: \r\n 314: ```json\r\n 315: {\r\n 316:   \"error\": {\r\n 317:     \"code\": \"UNAUTHORIZED\",\r\n 318:     \"message\": \"A valid authentication token is required.\"\r\n 319:   }\r\n 320: }\r\n 321: ```\r\n 322: \r\n 323: The error response deliberately does not reveal whether a token was malformed, expired, revoked or associated with a missing user.\r\n 324: \r\n 325: ### Authenticated but forbidden\r\n 326: \r\n 327: ```http\r\n 328: HTTP/1.1 403 Forbidden\r\n 329: ```\r\n 330: \r\n 331: ```json\r\n 332: {\r\n 333:   \"error\": {\r\n 334:     \"code\": \"FORBIDDEN\",\r\n 335:     \"message\": \"The authenticated account is not permitted to perform this operation.\"\r\n 336:   }\r\n 337: }\r\n 338: ```\r\n 339: \r\n 340: ## Running the backend\r\n 341: \r\n 342: Install dependencies:\r\n 343: \r\n 344: ```powershell\r\n 345: npm.cmd install\r\n 346: ```\r\n 347: \r\n 348: Copy the environment example:\r\n 349: \r\n 350: ```powershell\r\n 351: Copy-Item apps/backend/.env.example apps/backend/.env\r\n 352: ```\r\n 353: \r\n 354: Replace only the placeholders in the ignored `.env` file.\r\n 355: \r\n 356: Start the backend:\r\n 357: \r\n 358: ```powershell\r\n 359: npm.cmd run dev:backend\r\n 360: ```\r\n 361: \r\n 362: Verify the health endpoint:\r\n 363: \r\n 364: ```powershell\r\n 365: Invoke-RestMethod -Uri \"http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/v1/health\"\r\n 366: ```\r\n 367: \r\n 368: ## Unauthenticated proof\r\n 369: \r\n 370: Call the protected endpoint without a token:\r\n 371: \r\n 372: ```powershell\r\n 373: curl.exe -i http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/v1/auth/me\r\n 374: ```\r\n 375: \r\n 376: Expected result:\r\n 377: \r\n 378: ```text\r\n 379: HTTP/1.1 401 Unauthorized\r\n 380: WWW-Authenticate: Bearer\r\n 381: ```\r\n 382: \r\n 383: ## Authenticated development proof\r\n 384: \r\n 385: Use a disposable identity in the shared development Supabase project.\r\n 386: \r\n 387: Obtain an access token through a managed Supabase Auth sign-in flow. Keep the token only in memory and do not print, save, screenshot or commit it.\r\n 388: \r\n 389: Call the backend with:\r\n 390: \r\n 391: ```powershell\r\n 392: curl.exe -i `\r\n 393:   -H \"Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TEMPORARY_ACCESS_TOKEN\" `\r\n 394:   http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/v1/auth/me\r\n 395: ```\r\n 396: \r\n 397: Expected result:\r\n 398: \r\n 399: ```text\r\n 400: HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\n 401: ```\r\n 402: \r\n 403: The response must contain the synchronized application profile. Verify the authoritative role and\r\n 404: scope against the database rather than against token claims, deprecated request state, or frontend\r\n 405: state.\r\n 406: \r\n 407: Replace the token immediately after use and clear it from shell history where practical. Never include the real token in test evidence.\r\n 408: \r\n 409: ## Automated tests\r\n 410: \r\n 520: The backend enforces those rules from application database state. Administrator approval\r\n 521: management, submitter access requests and event-submission routes remain separate workflows.\r\n 522: \r\n 523: ## Security requirements\r\n 524: \r\n 525: - Use HTTPS in deployed environments.\r\n 526: - Never log bearer access tokens.\r\n 527: - Redact the `Authorization` header from request logs.\r\n 528: - Keep Google OAuth client secrets in provider dashboards.\r\n 529: - Never expose Supabase secret or `service_role` keys in frontend code.\r\n 530: - Keep real environment files ignored.\r\n 531: - Commit placeholders only.\r\n 532: - Configure exact redirect URLs and CORS origins.\r\n 533: - Use separate development and production configuration.\r\n 534: - Return safe authentication errors.\r\n 535: - Fail closed when persisted role or request-state values are unsupported.\r\n 536: - Never accept role, request state, or granted competition scopes from a request or token claim.\r\n 537: - Apply rate limiting before exposing sensitive production endpoints.\r\n 538: - Define Row Level Security and backend authorisation separately.\r\n 539: - Rotate credentials immediately if exposure is suspected.\r\n 540: \r\n 541: ## Deferred scope\r\n 542: \r\n 543: This foundation intentionally does not implement:\r\n 544: \r\n 545: - application-owned password-reset screens while Google OAuth remains the only sign-in method;\r\n 546: - event correction and file or batch upload interfaces;\r\n 547: - season or fixture scopes beyond reusable competition resolution;\r\n 548: - sport-specific authorisation;\r\n 549: - production Row Level Security policies.\r\n 550: \r\n 551: ## References\r\n 552: \r\n 553: - [Supabase Auth](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth)\r\n 554: - [Auth architecture](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/architecture)\r\n 555: - [Google login](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/social-login/auth-google)\r\n 556: - [getUser](https://supabase.com/docs/reference/javascript/auth-getuser)\r\n 557: - [Password authentication](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/passwords)\r\n 558: - [Administrative user deletion](https://supabase.com/docs/reference/javascript/auth-admin-deleteuser)\r\n 559: - [API keys](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/getting-started/api-keys)\r\n 560: - [Supabase CLI](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/local-development/cli/getting-started)\r\n 561: \r\n 562: ## AI Declaration\r\n 563: \r\n 564: The preceding document was planned and generated with the assistance of Codex[GPT-5]. The\r\n 565: frontend session-state and authenticated-request sections were later updated with the assistance of\r\n 566: Codex[GPT-5.6 Sol]. The account synchronization, profile, and authorization sections were updated\r\n 567: with the assistance of Codex[GPT-5.6 Sol]. The protected event-submission journey was documented\r\n 568: with the assistance of Codex[GPT-5.6 Sol].\r\n 569: The submitter access-request section was documented with the assistance of ChatGPT-Web[GPT-5.6 Sol].\r\n 570: The account-deletion security and recovery flow was documented with the assistance of Codex[GPT-5].\r\n 571: The submitter access-request frontend workflow was documented with the assistance of Codex[GPT-5].\r\nFILE docs/security/roles-and-permissions.md\r\n  47: ## Deprecated approval state\r\n  48: \r\n  49: `app_user.submitter_approval_state` is temporarily retained to support the existing access-request\r\n  50: workflow and migration history. It is deprecated as an authorization source. Approving a request\r\n  51: must assign `application_role = submitter` and the intended competition scopes through a trusted\r\n  52: administrative process. The column should be removed in a later migration once no workflow depends\r\n  53: on it.\r\n  54: \r\n  55: ## Administrator submitter workflow\r\n  56: \r\n  57: Only an account whose authoritative role is `admin` may call the user-management endpoints. The\r\n  58: backend does not accept an administrator role from token claims or profile metadata; it uses the\r\n  59: synchronized PostgreSQL account attached by authentication middleware.\r\n  60: \r\n  61: Approval and scope assignment are one operation. An approval is valid only when at least one\r\n  62: requested competition exists. In one transaction the backend:\r\n  63: \r\n  64: 1. locks and checks the target account;\r\n  65: 2. validates every requested competition;\r\n  66: 3. assigns `application_role = submitter` and the compatibility state `approved`;\r\n  67: 4. replaces the target account's complete `submitter_competition_scope`; and\r\n  68: 5. records the administrator and change time.\r\n  69: \r\n  70: Rejection and revocation are distinct. Rejection is permitted only for a pending viewer request; it\r\n  71: keeps `application_role = viewer`, records `rejected`, removes every competition scope, and permits a\r\n  72: later request. Revocation is permitted only for an approved submitter; it assigns\r\n  73: `application_role = viewer`, retains the historical `approved` request decision, and removes every\r\n  74: competition scope. Scope replacement is likewise limited to an approved submitter. Each transition\r\n  75: updates role, request state, scopes, administrator attribution, and time in one transaction.\r\n  76: \r\n  77: Invalid lifecycle changes return `409 INVALID_SUBMITTER_ACCESS_TRANSITION`. Validation and state\r\n  78: checks occur while the target row is locked, so a failed transition leaves the existing role,\r\n  79: request state, scopes, and audit fields unchanged.\r\n  80: \r\n  81: The submitter-access operation cannot modify an `admin` account, a disabled account, or the acting\r\n  82: administrator's own account. A viewer or submitter receives `403 Forbidden` before request-body\r\n  83: processing and therefore cannot approve themselves.\r\n  84: \r\n  85: `app_user.submitter_access_updated_by` and `submitter_access_updated_at` identify the most recent\r\n  86: administrator access change. The actor foreign key uses `ON DELETE SET NULL`: the change time\r\n  87: remains visible if the administrator account is later removed.\r\n  88: \r\n  89: ## Security boundary\r\n  90: \r\n  91: - The backend reads roles and scopes from PostgreSQL after verifying identity.\r\n  92: - Registration and profile synchronization never accept an application role from the request or\r\n  93:   from Supabase user metadata.\r\n  94: - Re-authentication may refresh the display name and last-authenticated time, but it never updates\r\n  95:   the persisted role or scopes.\r\n  96: - Only a trusted administrative backend process may change `application_role`.\r\n  97: - Approval, rejection, scope replacement, audit attribution, and revocation are committed atomically.\r\n  98: - Frontend visibility checks may improve the interface, but they are never the security boundary.\r\nFILE docs/api/overview.md\r\n  50: Authorization: Bearer <supabase-access-token>\r\n  51: ```\r\n  52: \r\n  53: Successful response:\r\n  54: \r\n  55: ```json\r\n  56: {\r\n  57:   \"user\": {\r\n  58:     \"id\": \"42\",\r\n  59:     \"subject\": \"<supabase-user-id>\",\r\n  60:     \"displayName\": \"Example User\",\r\n  61:     \"role\": \"submitter\",\r\n  62:     \"approvalState\": \"approved\",\r\n  63:     \"competitionIds\": [\"7\", \"12\"]\r\n  64:   }\r\n  65: }\r\n  66: ```\r\n  67: \r\n  68: Missing, malformed, invalid, expired or revoked tokens receive:\r\n  69: \r\n  70: ```http\r\n  71: HTTP/1.1 401 Unauthorized\r\n  72: WWW-Authenticate: Bearer\r\n  73: ```\r\n  74: \r\n  75: The API verifies the Supabase identity, creates or synchronizes the local account, and returns\r\n  76: server-owned authorization state. Authentication does not promote a user, approve submission, or\r\n  77: grant competition scope. A disabled account receives `403 Forbidden`.\r\n  78: \r\n  79: ### Account deletion\r\n  80: \r\n  81: An authenticated user can permanently delete their own account through:\r\n  82: \r\n  83: ```http\r\n  84: DELETE /api/v1/account\r\n  85: Authorization: Bearer <supabase-access-token>\r\n  86: Content-Type: application/json\r\n  87: \r\n  88: {\"confirmation\":\"DELETE\"}\r\n  89: ```\r\n  90: \r\n  91: The endpoint accepts no target account identifier, requires a sign-in no more than 15 minutes old,\r\n  92: and returns `422` unless the confirmation is exactly `DELETE`. A successful request disables the\r\n  93: local account, revokes its role, approval and competition grants, hard-deletes the Supabase Auth\r\n  94: user, and replaces local identity fields with a tombstone.\r\n  95: \r\n  96: The backend requires a separate server-only `SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY` for that provider operation. If it\r\n  97: is not configured, the route returns `501 ACCOUNT_DELETION_UNAVAILABLE` before changing local state;\r\n  98: other routes and health checks remain available.\r\n  99: \r\n 100: Submissions, fixtures, deliveries, derived statistics and their stable `app_user_id` provenance\r\n 101: remain. If Auth deletion or local finalization fails, the API returns `503`; the account remains\r\n 102: disabled and retry is idempotent. See [Privacy and retention](../security/privacy-retention.md).\r\n 103: \r\n 104: Application registration is handled by Supabase Auth; there is no backend registration or profile\r\n 105: mutation endpoint that accepts `application_role`. New application accounts are synchronized as\r\n 106: `viewer`, and only a trusted administrative backend process may change the role to `submitter` or\r\n 107: `admin`.\r\n 108: \r\n 109: ### Submitter access requests\r\n 110: \r\n 111: An authenticated viewer who does not already hold a submission-capable role can request submitter\r\n 112: access through:\r\n 113: \r\n 114: ```http\r\n 115: POST /api/v1/submitter-access-requests\r\n 116: Authorization: Bearer <supabase-access-token>\r\n 117: ```\r\n 118: \r\n 119: A successful request changes the authenticated application account's server-owned approval state to `pending`:\r\n 120: \r\n 121: ```json\r\n 122: {\r\n 123:   \"data\": {\r\n 124:     \"accountId\": \"42\",\r\n 125:     \"approvalState\": \"pending\"\r\n 126:   }\r\n 127: }\r\n 128: ```\r\n 129: \r\n 130: The endpoint returns `401 Unauthorized` when no valid authentication is supplied.\r\n 131: \r\n 132: A `409 Conflict` is returned when the account already has a pending request, has the legacy\r\n 133: `approved` request state, or already holds the `submitter`/`admin` role. A previously rejected\r\n 134: viewer may submit a new request.\r\n 135: \r\n 136: The request state is stored on the provider-neutral application account and can subsequently be\r\n 137: consumed by the administrator approval and competition-scope workflow. The request state is not an\r\n 138: authorization grant: approval must assign `application_role = submitter`, and the backend uses that\r\n 139: role plus competition scope for submission decisions.\r\n 140: \r\n 141: ### Administrator submitter-access decisions\r\n 142: \r\n 143: Administrators can list application users through:\r\n 144: \r\n 145: ```http\r\n 146: GET /api/v1/admin/users\r\n 147: Authorization: Bearer <supabase-access-token>\r\n 148: ```\r\n 149: \r\n 150: The endpoint is restricted to the `admin` role and provides the account state needed for\r\n 151: submitter-access review and administration.\r\n 152: \r\n 153: Only an authoritative `admin` may manage another active, non-administrator account. Approval and\r\n 154: scope replacement use `PATCH /api/v1/admin/users/{userId}/submitter-access`; approval requires a\r\n 155: pending viewer and at least one valid competition scope, while scope replacement requires an\r\n 156: approved submitter. Sending `approved: false` revokes an approved submitter, removes every scope,\r\n 157: and retains the historical `approved` request decision.\r\n 158: \r\n 159: Rejecting a pending request is a separate action:\r\n 160: \r\n 161: ```http\r\n 162: POST /api/v1/admin/users/{userId}/submitter-access/rejection\r\n 163: Authorization: Bearer <supabase-access-token>\r\n 164: ```\r\n 165: \r\n 166: Rejection keeps the account as a viewer, writes `rejected`, clears all scopes, and allows the user\r\n 167: to request again. Invalid lifecycle changes return `409 INVALID_SUBMITTER_ACCESS_TRANSITION` and\r\n 168: leave the account's role, request state, scopes, and audit fields unchanged.\r\n 169: \r\n 170: ### Public read\r\n 171: \r\n 172: The following endpoints are available without authentication:\r\n 173: \r\n 174: ```text\r\n 175: GET /api/v1/competitions\r\nFILE docs/api/contracts.md\r\n  70: A stable identifier must be used as the final tie-breaker\r\n  71: so pagination remains deterministic.\r\n  72: \r\n  73: Event endpoints always use event occurrence order.\r\n  74: \r\n  75: Administrator submitter access\r\n  76: ------------------------------\r\n  77: \r\n  78: `GET /api/v1/admin/users` and\r\n  79: `PATCH /api/v1/admin/users/{userId}/submitter-access` require the authoritative `admin` role. A\r\n  80: pending request is rejected through the separate administrator-only\r\n  81: `POST /api/v1/admin/users/{userId}/submitter-access/rejection` action.\r\n  82: \r\n  83: New approval and rejection decisions require the target to be a `viewer` with a persisted `pending`\r\n  84: request. A `not_requested` or `rejected` viewer receives\r\n  85: `409 INVALID_SUBMITTER_ACCESS_TRANSITION` and must create a new request before approval. Existing\r\n  86: approved submitters may still be re-scoped or revoked through the PATCH endpoint.\r\n  87: \r\n  88: Approval replaces the complete competition scope and requires one or more unique, existing\r\n  89: competition identifiers:\r\n  90: \r\n  91: ```json\r\n  92: {\r\n  93:   \"approved\": true,\r\n  94:   \"competitionIds\": [\"12\", \"18\"]\r\n  95: }\r\n  96: ```\r\n  97: \r\n  98: Revocation always sends an empty scope:\r\n  99: \r\n 100: ```json\r\n 101: {\r\n 102:   \"approved\": false,\r\n 103:   \"competitionIds\": []\r\n 104: }\r\n 105: ```\r\n 106: \r\n 107: Approval is valid only for a `pending` viewer. Scope replacement and revocation are valid only for\r\n 108: an existing `approved` submitter. Revocation removes the `submitter` role and all scopes while\r\n 109: retaining the historical `approved` request decision. Rejection applies only to a `pending` viewer,\r\n 110: keeps the `viewer` role, writes `rejected`, and removes all scopes. A rejected viewer may request\r\n 111: access again.\r\n 112: \r\n 113: The returned user includes the effective role, compatibility approval state, named competition\r\n 114: scopes, and the latest submitter-access change actor/time. `403` means the caller is not an\r\n 115: administrator; malformed or nonexistent scopes return `422`; protected, self-targeted, or invalid\r\n 116: lifecycle changes return `409`. Invalid lifecycle changes use the stable\r\n 117: `INVALID_SUBMITTER_ACCESS_TRANSITION` code and do not change role, request state, scopes, or audit\r\n 118: fields.\r\n 119: \r\n 120: Errors\r\n 121: â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€â”€\r\n 122: {\r\n 123: \"error\": {\r\n 124: \"code\": \"VALIDATION_FAILED\",\r\n 125: \"message\": \"The request is invalid.\",\r\nFILE apps/backend/README.md\r\nArgument types do not match\r\nAt line:2 char:1037\r\n+ ... or ($i=$range[0]; $i -le [Math]::Min($range[1],$lines.Length); $i++)  ...\r\n+                       ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\r\n    + CategoryInfo          : OperationStopped: (:) [], ArgumentException\r\n    + FullyQualifiedErrorId : System.ArgumentException\r\n \r\nArgument types do not match\r\nAt line:2 char:1037\r\n+ ... or ($i=$range[0]; $i -le [Math]::Min($range[1],$lines.Length); $i++)  ...\r\n+                       ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\r\n    + CategoryInfo          : OperationStopped: (:) [], ArgumentException\r\n    + FullyQualifiedErrorId : System.ArgumentException\r\n \r\nFILE apps/frontend/README.md\r\nArgument types do not match\r\nAt line:2 char:1037\r\n+ ... or ($i=$range[0]; $i -le [Math]::Min($range[1],$lines.Length); $i++)  ...\r\n+                       ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\r\n    + CategoryInfo          : OperationStopped: (:) [], ArgumentException\r\n    + FullyQualifiedErrorId : System.ArgumentException\r\n \r\nArgument types do not match\r\nAt line:2 char:1037\r\n+ ... or ($i=$range[0]; $i -le [Math]::Min($range[1],$lines.Length); $i++)  ...\r\n+                       ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\r\n    + CategoryInfo       