evidence\ai\transcripts\dean-feldman\2026-08-26-issue-255-competition-scoped-submitter-access.jsonl:824:{"timestamp":"2026-08-26T18:48:09.097Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a03f66-ae89-7b60-9934-a5c8b8f30a0b","call_id":"call_MJgNSe602vrbViSjb533bJVf","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"# AI usage registers\r\n\r\nEach team member records their AI usage in their own file under `registers/`.\r\nThe file is named to match the member''s transcript folder under `transcripts/`.\r\n\r\nThe single shared register at `ai-usage-register.csv` is being retired. It was\r\nedited by all six members, conflicted on almost every merge, and was corrupted\r\nthree times. Entries remain in both files until every member has created their\r\nown, after which the shared register is removed.\r\n\r\n## Columns\r\n\r\n| Column                     | Content                                                            |\r\n| -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\r\n| Date                       | The date the work was done, as `YYYY-MM-DD`.                       |\r\n| Team member                | The member who used the tool.                                      |\r\n| Tool                       | The tool used, for example `Claude Web` or `ChatGPT Web`.          |\r\n| Model                      | The model used, for example `Claude Opus 5` or `GPT-5.6 Thinking`. |\r\n| Purpose                    | The kinds of use, separated by semicolons.                         |\r\n| Brief task                 | What the work was, including the issue number where applicable.    |\r\n| Output used                | What was produced and actually used.                               |\r\n| Verification or adaptation | How the output was checked, tested or changed before use.          |\r\n| Related evidence           | Transcript paths, issue numbers and Pull Requests.                 |\r\n\r\n## Rules\r\n\r\nEvery field must be quoted, including the date. Fields contain commas and\r\nsemicolons, and an unquoted field containing a comma breaks the file for every\r\nreader.\r\n\r\nEvery file must end with a trailing newline. A row appended to a file without one\r\nis fused onto the row above it.\r\n\r\nUse a semicolon, not a comma, to separate items within a field.\r\n\r\nOnly tools and models genuinely used may be listed. The register is a record of\r\nwhat happened, not a declaration of what was permitted.\r\n\r\n## Relationship to other attribution\r\n\r\nThis register does not replace the attribution the course AI policy requires\r\nelsewhere. Commits containing AI-generated code carry an `Assisted-by` line,\r\ndocuments carry an AI declaration, and the repository README declares the tools\r\nin use.\r\n\r\n## AI Declaration\r\n\r\nThe preceding document was generated with the assistance of Claude-Web[Claude Opus 5].\r\n\"2026-08-18\",\"Dean Feldman\",\"Codex\",\"GPT-5\",\"Backend implementation; authorization state-machine hardening; PostgreSQL transaction safety; automated testing; API and security documentation\",\"Issue #182: add explicit administrator rejection of pending submitter-access requests and enforce valid approval, scope-management, rejection, and revocation transitions.\",\"Dedicated rejection API; locked-row lifecycle policy; stable invalid-transition conflict; atomic role, approval-state, audit, and competition-scope updates; unit, API, and PostgreSQL regression coverage; aligned OpenAPI and security documentation.\",\"Reviewed the persisted role and approval-state matrix; passed the complete npm run check with 72 backend unit tests, 68 frontend tests, 81 API tests, 68 contract tests, 4 deployment-helper tests, OpenAPI lint and all production builds; then reset, migrated, seeded, and passed all 33 tests in 7 database files against a disposable PostgreSQL 16 cluster.\",\"Issue #182; branch feat/182-submitter-access-transitions; evidence/validation/issue-182-submitter-access-transitions.md\"\r\n\"2026-08-18\",\"Dean Feldman\",\"Codex\",\"GPT-5\",\"Testing infrastructure; PostgreSQL integration; automated verification; documentation; local development troubleshooting\",\"Issue #68: complete and verify the Basic vertical-slice test coverage, remove the local PostgreSQL execution blocker, and repair the shared-checkout WSL native dependencies.\",\"Disposable PostgreSQL 16 test runner; explicit database-test and CI paths; migration-process cleanup; CI, testing, environment, backend, dependency, and validation documentation; local WSL Rollup/esbuild repair.\",\"Mapped every acceptance criterion to existing automated coverage; ran 30 PostgreSQL tests against the disposable cluster; passed the database-independent npm run check with 56 backend unit, 65 frontend, 76 API, 68 contract, and 4 deployment tests plus all static checks and builds; started both frontend and backend successfully in WSL after the ignored node_modules repair.\",\"Issue #68; branch test/68-basic-vertical-slice; evidence/validation/issue-68-basic-vertical-slice.md; Pull Request #183\"\r\n\"2026-08-18\",\"Dean Feldman\",\"Codex\",\"GPT-5\",\"Git conflict resolution; testing infrastructure reconciliation; automated verification; documentation\",\"Pull Request #183: merge current main and resolve overlaps between the Basic vertical-slice embedded PostgreSQL runner and the merged Issue #176 Docker workflow.\",\"Resolved five content conflicts; retained the embedded database runner as an explicit command outside the normal quality gate; preserved the Docker alternative and explicit CI database step; separated reset and migration responsibilities; reconciled README, setup, testing, and technology documentation.\",\"Ran npm run check successfully with 67 backend unit tests, 68 frontend tests, 77 API tests, 68 contract tests, 4 deployment-helper tests, OpenAPI lint, 